<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mabo v Queensland (No 2)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Mabo decision</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mabo v Queensland (No 2) was a 1992 decision of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-court-of-australia">High Court of Australia</a> in a case brought by Eddie Mabo and other Meriam people of the Torres Strait. The court rejected the doctrine that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> had been terra nullius, land belonging to no one, at British settlement, and recognised native title. Parliament followed with the Native Title Act of 1993.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The case concerned the Murray Islands Group, Mer, Waua Islet and Daua Island, home of the Meriam people. Land on Mer is held under Malo's Law, a set of religiously sanctioned laws under which the whole island belongs to Meriam owners, with no public ownership; land passes orally, held by the eldest son on behalf of a lineage. The arrival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> in 1871 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> without displacing these customs, and Queensland annexed the islands in 1879.</p><p>Australian law had not recognised pre-colonial property interests: in Milirrpum v Nabalco Pty Ltd in the 1970s native title was held never to have existed. In 1982 the plaintiffs, led by Eddie Mabo and represented by the barrister Ron Castan, asked the High Court to declare the Meriam owners of their land. Queensland's attempt to extinguish any such title by the Queensland Coast Islands Declaratory Act 1985 was struck down in Mabo No 1 in 1988 as inconsistent with the Racial Discrimination Act 1975.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Decided on 3 June 1992, the judgment held that native title exists as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, that its source is the traditional laws and customs of Indigenous groups rather than a grant from the Crown, and that its content depends on those continuing laws and customs. The Crown acquired radical title with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, but that did not by itself extinguish native title, which could be ended only by a valid exercise of government power inconsistent with it. Justice Brennan endorsed a three-part test of Indigenous identity: biological descent from the people, the person's own recognition of membership, and recognition by the elders or others holding traditional authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-keating">Paul Keating</a> praised the decision in his Redfern Speech as establishing a fundamental truth, while Richard Court, premier of Western Australia, and mining and pastoral groups opposed it. The Native Title Act 1993 set up the National Native Title Tribunal to hear claims, and the Howard government later amended it after the Wik decision. In 2005 the historian Michael Connor argued in The Invention of Terra Nullius that the case was wrongly decided, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had annexed Australia rather than treating it as terra nullius. Brennan's test of Indigenous identity has since been used in later cases, among them Love v Commonwealth. Mabo Day, 3 June, is a holiday in the Torres Shire.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mabo v Queensland (No 2)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The High Court’s <b>Mabo decision</b> of 1992, which rejected the doctrine that the land had been terra nullius at British colonisation, transformed the law of Indigenous land rights, and Aboriginal art has become one of the most widely recognised contemporary art movements.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
